Yes, I can provide a sample Java code for implementing a sentiment analysis algorithm that rephrases negative statements to positive ones using an Excel table of positive words and synonyms. Here is a possible solution:
```java
import java.io.File;
import java.io.FileInputStream;
import java.io.IOException;
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;
import org.apache.poi.ss.usermodel.Cell;
import org.apache.poi.ss.usermodel.Row;
import org.apache.poi.ss.usermodel.Sheet;
import org.apache.poi.ss.usermodel.Workbook;
import org.apache.poi.ss.usermodel.WorkbookFactory;
public class SentimentAnalyzer {

  private Map<String, String> synonyms;

  public SentimentAnalyzer(String excelFilePath) throws IOException {
    // Load the Excel file containing the positive words and synonyms
    FileInputStream inputStream = new FileInputStream(new File(excelFilePath));
    Workbook workbook = WorkbookFactory.create(inputStream);
    Sheet sheet = workbook.getSheetAt(0);
    synonyms = new HashMap<>();
    // Iterate over the rows of the sheet and store the synonyms in a map
    for (Row row : sheet) {
      Cell cell1 = row.getCell(0);
      Cell cell2 = row.getCell(1);
      if (cell1 != null && cell2 != null) {
        String word = cell1.getStringCellValue().toLowerCase();
        String synonym = cell2.getStringCellValue().toLowerCase();
        synonyms.put(word, synonym);
      }
    }
    workbook.close();
    inputStream.close();
  }

  public String rephrase(String sentence) {
    // Tokenize the sentence by splitting it into words
    String[] words = sentence.toLowerCase().split("\\s+");
    // Iterate over the words and replace negative ones with positive synonyms if available
    StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
    for (String word : words) {
      if (synonyms.containsKey(word)) {
        sb.append(synonyms.get(word)).append(" ");
      } else {
        sb.append(word).append(" ");
      }
    }
    // Capitalize the first letter of the sentence and add a period
    String rephrased = sb.toString().trim();
    if (!rephrased.isEmpty()) {
      rephrased = Character.toUpperCase(rephrased.charAt(0)) + rephrased.substring(1);
      rephrased += ".";
    }
    return rephrased;
  }

  public static void main(String[] args) throws IOException {
    SentimentAnalyzer analyzer = new SentimentAnalyzer("positive_words.xlsx");
    String input = "I'm too tired to do anything productive today";
    String output = analyzer.rephrase(input);
    System.out.println("Input: " + input);
    System.out.println("Output: " + output);
  }
}
```
In this code, the `SentimentAnalyzer` class has a constructor that takes an Excel file path as input and loads the positive words and synonyms into a `Map<String, String>` called `synonyms`. The `rephrase` method takes a sentence as input, tokenizes it into words, and replaces negative words with their positive synonyms if available. Finally, it capitalizes the first letter of the sentence and adds a period.
In the `main` method, we create a `SentimentAnalyzer` object with the path to the Excel file containing the positive words and synonyms, and test it with a sample input sentence. The expected output for the given input sentence is "I'm too energized to do anything productive today."

