To modify the C++ code to sort an array in descending order using a selection sort algorithm, we need to change the comparison operator in the inner loop. Instead of checking if the current element is less than the minimum element, we need to check if it is greater than the maximum element. If it is, we update the index of the maximum element.
Here is the modified code:
```
void selectionSort(int arr[], int n) {
    int i, j, max_idx;
    for (i = 0; i < n-1; i++) {
        max_idx = i;
        for (j = i+1; j < n; j++) {
            if (arr[j] > arr[max_idx]) {
                max_idx = j;
            }
        }
        int temp = arr[i];
        arr[i] = arr[max_idx];
        arr[max_idx] = temp;
    }
}
```
The time complexity of the selection sort algorithm is O(n^2) in both the best and worst case scenarios. This is because there are two nested loops that iterate over the entire array, resulting in n*(n-1)/2 comparisons and swaps. Despite its simplicity, selection sort is not very efficient for large datasets and is generally only used for educational purposes.

